# Palindrome over {a,b}. Answers yes or no.
#
#   rw progs/palin.rw '<abba>'   ->  yes
#   rw progs/palin.rw '<abb>'    ->  no
#
# The tape has no random access and the rules have no variables, so the check
# is done by eating the string from both ends: pick the first letter up into
# the marker that carries it (A carries an a, B carries a b), walk that marker
# to the far end, and see what it meets there. Match, and both letters go;
# mismatch, and the answer is already known.
#
# Rule order carries the invariant: picking a letter up is the last resort, so
# every rule that mentions a marker sits above it. Otherwise a tape like <bA>
# matches "<b" and picks up a second letter while the first is still in
# flight, and the program is only correct with one marker alive.

# nothing left, or one letter stranded in the middle: every pair agreed
<>->.yes
<a>->.yes
<b>->.yes

# a mismatch was found; W eats what is left so the answer stands on its own
aW->W
bW->W
<W->.no

# the far end: the carried letter meets the last one
aA>->>
bB>->>
bA>->W
aB>->W

# carrying a letter rightwards
Aa->aA
Ab->bA
Ba->aB
Bb->bB

# last resort: pick the first letter up. Below the answers too, or a lone
# letter would be carried off into an empty tape instead of saying yes.
<a-><A
<b-><B
